# ClickUp Simplifier — Cursor AI Rules

## Project overview

A personal, keyboard-driven desktop/web client that replaces the ClickUp web UI
for a single power user. The app works on a **local copy** of ClickUp data and
syncs selectively back to ClickUp. Speed, keyboard predictability, and a lean
interface are the core promises. PRD: `context/foundation/prd.md`.

## Domain model — non-negotiable rules

1. **Milestone → Task hierarchy is strict.** Milestones and tasks are NEVER
   rendered or stored at the same level. Every task belongs to exactly one
   milestone. Tasks without a milestone go under a virtual "no milestone" node —
   they are never hidden.

2. **Named sync sets.** Synchronization is split into exactly two named sets for
   the MVP:
   - *Podstawowe słowniki* — dictionaries (spaces, folders, lists); slow cadence.
   - *Zadania* — tasks; fast, incremental cadence.
   Each set independently tracks `lastSuccess`, `lastFailure`, and `errorDescription`.
   A later failure does NOT erase the last-known-good timestamp.

3. **Write-back is explicit, never automatic.** Local changes reach ClickUp only
   after the user reviews and approves a pending-changes queue. No silent auto-push.

4. **ClickUp auth = personal API token only.** No OAuth for MVP. The token is
   stored on-device; treat it as a secret (never log, never embed in source).

5. **Single user, no app-level auth.** The app has no login screen. One person,
   one device.

## Functional requirements summary (FR reference)

| ID     | Description                                              | Priority    |
|--------|----------------------------------------------------------|-------------|
| FR-001 | Store ClickUp personal API token in settings             | must-have   |
| FR-002 | Pull whole workspace into local copy (one-time full, then incremental) | must-have |
| FR-003 | Named sync sets with independent cadences                | must-have   |
| FR-004 | On-demand sync trigger per set                           | must-have   |
| FR-005 | Context selector (folder / space scope)                  | must-have   |
| FR-006 | Restore last context on launch                           | nice-to-have|
| FR-007 | Full keyboard operability — no operation requires a mouse| must-have   |
| FR-008 | Strict milestone→task nesting in UI                      | must-have   |
| FR-009 | Create milestone                                         | must-have   |
| FR-010 | Assign task to milestone                                 | must-have   |
| FR-011 | View milestone release note (list of resolved tasks)     | nice-to-have|
| FR-012 | Change task status / mark complete                       | must-have   |
| FR-013 | Edit task title and description                          | must-have   |
| FR-014 | Reviewed write-back to ClickUp (explicit approve step)   | must-have   |
| FR-015 | Sync management panel listing all sync sets              | must-have   |
| FR-016 | Show lastSuccess + lastFailure timestamp per sync set    | must-have   |
| FR-017 | Show error description when last run failed              | must-have   |
| FR-018 | Immediate on-demand trigger from sync panel              | must-have   |
| FR-019 | Per-set auto-sync frequency (presets + custom input)     | must-have   |

## Non-functional constraints

- Navigation and read operations on the local copy: **≤ 100 ms** perceived latency,
  even at full-workspace scale.
- Keyboard actions must be deterministic: same key, same result, every time.
  **No surprise focus jumps** — focus moves only as a direct result of a user action.
- Changes to ClickUp only after explicit user approval (write-back safety).
- Task changes in ClickUp reflected in local copy **within minutes** when connected.

## Non-goals (do NOT implement)

- Multi-user, roles, sharing, collaboration
- ClickUp feature parity (comments, attachments, Gantt, automations, dashboards)
- Creating/editing spaces, folders, or lists (dictionaries are read-only)
- Offline operation
- OAuth flow
- User-defined custom sync sets (catalogue is fixed at two for MVP)

## Tech stack status

Stack is **deliberately deferred** — multiple parallel explorations:
- Native web
- Flutter (web + desktop target)
- Native desktop in Java

The architecture must support a **shared core** (local DB + sync engine + domain
model) with **swappable frontends**. When adding code, keep business logic out of
UI layers so clients can be swapped.

## Open questions (do not assume answers)

1. How are tasks with no milestone node presented in the hierarchy? (virtual node proposed, not locked)
2. Exact UX for the write-back review step (pending-changes queue + confirm — shape TBD)
3. Predefined preset cadences for FR-019 (values not yet chosen)

## Coding guidelines

- No comments explaining *what* code does — names should do that. Only comment
  *why* when there is a hidden constraint or non-obvious invariant.
- No premature abstractions. Three similar lines beat a helper added for hypothetical reuse.
- No error handling for impossible cases. Validate only at system boundaries
  (user input, ClickUp API responses).
- The ClickUp API token must never appear in logs, console output, or error messages.
- Sync state (lastSuccess/lastFailure/errorDescription) must survive app restart.
